## Decision

This record replaces the subject-only rule in [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject).
Keep that accepted record as history. Split #4471 into a shared-parser ticket, a booking-v3
hint ticket. Keep the five flagged reports in the parser ticket as investigation notes. Do not describe the whole ticket as one known bug
with one queued fix. The export contains support reports and KB logic, not the product source
or a working product; reproductions in our tickets are plans to verify, not tests we ran.

<ReviewSummary />

## Filed tickets

- [Parser · 379 reports](https://github.com/prophen/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1)
- [Form hint · 33 reports](https://github.com/prophen/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2)
- [Closed duplicate investigation #3](https://github.com/prophen/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/3): its five reports are included in parser #1.

## Why these are separate

The shared parser reads month first. Day-first tenants entering dates through app/email or
booking-v2 can get the month and day swapped. For example, harbour.dental report R-41200
describes 7 April becoming 4 July. Use the tenant directory and the actual input path;
the browser locale and intake tag are guesses. Both app and email can reach the same cause.

booking-v3 instead prints MM/DD/YYYY while its form reads dates in the tenant’s day-first
order. That misleading instruction is a separate defect affecting alder.physio, copper.studio,
and pale.harbour form reports. Fixing the shared parser alone does not correct the hint.
For the same tenant, a form report and an app report can have different causes.

The five desk-flagged reports remain in the parser group and need closer investigation within that ticket.
R-41202 and R-41620 describe adjacent days; R-41209 describes June-only shifts; R-41344
describes different days for Lisbon and Toronto. R-41236 says an invite arrived before the
booking, without giving timestamps. A timezone, DST or date-only conversion defect is plausible,
but the export does not establish a separate root cause. Request the original
timestamps, timezone IDs, inputs and invite/delivery logs. Create another ticket only if new evidence establishes a distinct cause. Verify these reported symptoms when validating the parser fix.

## Audit the code’s original grouping

This live table preserves what the supplied classifier actually computes. Its parser count
includes the five flagged reports; its footnote keeps their unusual symptoms visible. Intake labels
disagree with the code on 38 reports: 23 parser reports tagged hint and 15 hint reports tagged
parser. Counting tags would give the wrong split. Deduplicate IDs before counting anything.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## What the queue shows

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

The received and merged bars measure different events. Reports arrived from April onward.
The week beginning 29 June has a large merge bar because older reports were filed together;
it is not evidence that the defect started in July or that reports suddenly surged then.
The latest report arrived on 4 October. A reviewed date or a known label does not prove a fix.

## The desk’s next report

1. Read the body and capture tenant, product, input channel, form version, original input,
   expected date, actual date and timezone. Look up tenant locale; do not substitute browser locale.
2. Compare the failure mechanism with the candidate ticket. Merge only when evidence supports
   the same cause. Similar subjects can conceal different problems; different subjects can
   describe one problem. Subject matching may suggest a candidate but cannot decide a merge.
3. Keep unknown tenants, unexplained one-day shifts and missing evidence separate pending
   investigation. A missing cause is not a shared cause. Ask for evidence rather than merge by default.
4. Add each unique report ID once and retain its original received time and merge history.
   Update counts as reports arrive. Recheck both input paths after a fix; split or reopen on
   contradictory evidence. Do not close the other ticket merely because one fix shipped.

The old triage page was reviewed in June 2024, beyond the KB’s 180-day review window.
Re-read it against live views rather than trusting its accepted label. The September 2026
incident page is recent but contradicted by its own computed table. Fresh prose can still be wrong.

## Engineering outcome · 5 October

The course’s engineering update closes #4471 with two different outcomes:

- Parser: **fixed**, `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. Parser issue #1 is closed as completed.
- Hint: **won’t fix**, because booking-v3 was retired. Form-hint issue #2 is closed as not planned.

The hint was not fixed by the parser commit. Its affected form was retired, which is a different disposition.
The original 379/33 report counts remain historical evidence. Apply the updated triage rule to future reports;
a similar subject alone does not establish a recurrence. The update is engineering’s report, not a product test run by this harness.

